  "verdict": "On a real 113-country non-crisis-inclusive panel (LV_30_10 rule, theta=1, k=2y): the Guidotti–Greenspan signal (ratio>1) fires in 650/3451 country-years; 75% of firings are false alarms (no crash within 2y) and it misses 65% of crises (recall=0.354). Yet it is NOT noise: noise-to-signal=0.461 (<1) and lift=1.877 — a firing lifts 2-yr crisis odds from the 13% base rate to 25%. So the true false-POSITIVE rate the 5-country in-episode test (false_positive_test.py) structurally could not measure is now MEASURED: a genuine-but-weak, false-alarm-dominated single-threshold signal — simultaneously consistent with Frankel–Saravelos ('best reserve measure') and Berg–Borensztein–Pattillo ('false alarms are the rule'). §5.6's cautious rating stands, now on measured numbers rather than an absent one. The 65% of crises with ratio<=1 are the Malaysia-type false negative at panel scale.",
  "honest_limits": "WDI/DRS is NOT the BIS-based RBA end-June-1997 measure of §5.5 (Korea and Malaysia are absent here; overlapping values differ). The crash rules REPRODUCE the Frankel–Rose and Laeven–Valencia thresholds on WDI FX data; they are not L&V's verbatim curated list (edge cases differ). Country-years are serially correlated (effective N < count). A mechanical crash rule cannot detect crises averted by capital controls (Malaysia 1998), matching the single-channel caveat. High-inflation country-years can still trip the rule despite the acceleration term."
}